```python
import re

# Allowlist: only these characters, and a bounded length
USERNAME_PATTERN = re.compile(r'^[a-zA-Z0-9_]{3,20}$')

def validate_username(username: str) -> bool:
    return bool(USERNAME_PATTERN.match(username))

# Contrast: a blocklist approach, shown for why it's weaker
BLOCKED_CHARS = {"'", '"', ';', '<', '>'}

def validate_username_blocklist(username: str) -> bool:
    # Only catches the specific characters listed here —
    # anything else (unicode lookalikes, other special chars) passes through
    return not any(c in BLOCKED_CHARS for c in username)
```

The allowlist version rejects anything outside `a-z, A-Z, 0-9, underscore` in a 3-20 character window — complete by construction, regardless of what creative input someone tries. The blocklist version only catches the five characters someone thought to list; a unicode lookalike character, a different special character, or a multi-byte sequence that behaves unexpectedly downstream all sail straight through it.
